WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.367
-Our next guest is an Emmy
and Tony Award-winning actor

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:05.567
you know best from his work
in "Angels in America,"

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:07.400
"Westworld,"
and "Boardwalk Empire."

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.267
He stars in the highly
anticipated film "The Batman,"

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.534
which is in theaters and IMAX
this Friday.

00:00:12.534 --> 00:00:13.834
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:18.667
♪♪

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:22.100
-Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.968
Police section.

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:27.934
-He's with me, Officer.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:30.400
-Are you kidding me, sir?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.834
You're gonna let him in here?

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:36.000
-Martinez, let him through.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jeffrey Wright.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.801
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:54.734
♪♪

00:00:54.734 --> 00:01:00.734
♪♪

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:02.267
Welcome back.
-Yeah!

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.901
-I am very -- I'm not alone in
being very excited about

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.534
"The Batman," but I'm also
excited about this.

00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:08.000
You have three different
projects

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.534
about Frederick Douglass.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:10.968
You have an HBO series
and an audiobook,

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:13.734
which makes the most sense to me
in the world because you have

00:01:13.734 --> 00:01:16.200
maybe my favorite voice
of anyone working.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.567
-Well, thank you.
You know, I guess,

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:21.801
you know, things are happening
around us

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:27.200
at such a rapid and intense pace
that, you know, I don't know

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.834
about anybody else, but I feel
like Lucy, you know, trying to

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.667
wrap the chocolates,
trying to take it all in

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.467
and understand what's going on
in the world.

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:36.868
It's like, "Holy [bleep]

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:40.567
That's a lot of chocolates
coming at me at a rapid pace."

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:43.434
So what I find healthy is,
you know, to go back

00:01:43.434 --> 00:01:47.667
and look at the history of
things, shine some perspective

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.667
on events happening now in a way
that, you know, helps clarify.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:55.067
So went back and did a recording

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:57.834
of "The Narrative of the Life of
Frederick Douglass,"

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.968
which is kind of, I guess,
a companion piece

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:02.367
to the "Lincoln's Dilemma"
docuseries

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.067
that just aired on Apple.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.200
But this book is
just phenomenal.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.234
And it really gives us --
you know, in the midst of

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:13.167
all the race discourse that's
going on now,

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:16.033
it's really brilliant
perspective

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.667
on some of the drivers that have

00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:21.234
brought our country
to where it is.

00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:23.200
And Douglass is just brilliant,
just brilliant.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.767
So check that out.

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:29.133
It's really, you know -- because
look, Black History Month

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.133
just ended, so what better time
than now...

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.200
[ Laughter ]
...to check out Frederick?

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.033
'Cause it's American history,
after all, you know?

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.767
-It is American history.

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:41.467
The biggest mistake I think
anyone makes is slotting

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:43.901
black history into one month
when it's, you know --

00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:46.367
it's crazy to think that
it belongs in one place.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:49.200
-Yeah, 'cause, you know,
black folks didn't do

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.167
the slavery thing by themselves,
you know?

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:52.901
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.767
-It took a village...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:57.267
...as they say,
or it took a nation.

00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:58.767
So, yeah, so check that out,

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:01.767
if only for the appendix alone,
which is just stunning.

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:06.701
He, like -- He, like, dissects
the hypocrisy of slavery

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:10.767
in America like a surgeon
at a hog slaughter.

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:12.100
It's just unreal.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.934
It's just piercing.
So, well worth your time.

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:16.234
Well worth your listening.

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:18.534
-I'm very, very excited
to listen to that.

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:20.968
I'm wondering when you, you
know, you've obviously worked in

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:24.300
big franchises before --
"Bond" films, "Hunger Games" --

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.234
taking on "Batman," this is, you
know, there's such a fan base

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:29.734
and it seems a growing fan base
despite the fact

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:32.367
this is a character that's been
around since the late '30s.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:34.100
-Yeah.
-Do you have any anxiety

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.167
taking on a character like
Commissioner Jim Gordon,

00:03:36.167 --> 00:03:37.667
or is it just exciting?

00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:41.834
-Well, you know, you want to do
justice to that fan base

00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:43.601
and to the history,
to the legacy.

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:45.033
It is 1939.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:48.734
It's 80 years of evolution of
this thing.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.634
And I think because of that
but also there's something about

00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:54.868
Batman that, unlike some of
the other comic book heroes,

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:58.334
you know, he lives in a city
that looks very much like

00:03:58.334 --> 00:04:01.234
that city outside your window
there, Seth.

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:04.067
So he's grounded in a way

00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:05.400
that maybe the other characters
aren't.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.033
And it also allows for
an interesting examination of

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:13.434
the things, the issues that
exist in a city

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:14.934
like New York City,
which, of course,

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:18.100
was the basis for Gotham
in 1939.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.200
And so you've got that.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.834
You've got, you know, 80 years
of fans like, you know,

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:26.634
kind of like
diving into this thing

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:29.133
and they have a deeply personal
relationship to it.

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:31.601
Unlike, you know,
much of what --

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:33.901
I've been in some big franchises
but nothing like this.

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:35.534
It really is singular.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:39.434
I think, too, that people,
particularly at uncertain times,

00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:41.901
and we're kind of
in uncertain times now,

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:44.300
but I think people are yearning
for a place

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.200
to place their trust
and to find hope.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.734
And I think there's a real,
you know,

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:54.601
there's a powerful opportunity
for people to do those things

00:04:54.601 --> 00:04:56.501
through these characters
and through this film.

00:04:56.501 --> 00:04:58.501
It's not --
They don't take it lightly.

00:04:58.501 --> 00:04:59.934
I mean, they're intense.
They're like,

00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:02.167
"Hey, don't screw up with
my stuff, man.

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.701
Don't screw -- You know, do it
right, do it justice."

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:06.133
And so, yeah, there's
that pressure.

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:10.100
But I think what we really tried
to do was to focus on, you know,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.234
the essence of what these
characters are about

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:14.367
and what the franchise is about
and do that justice,

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:16.801
and in doing that,
do fans justice as well.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:19.300
-You talked about how it's based
on New York and, you know,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.567
we have a New York leader right
now, Mayor Eric Adams,

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:24.367
who you actually have known
for a good long time as well.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.000
-Yeah, I've known him
for a while.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.601
I first became aware of him
in the '90s.

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:32.801
You know, I was in New York, and
there were some pretty intense

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:35.501
police-brutality cases
in the city at that time.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:41.200
And one of -- and maybe the only
powerful voice in blue

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.033
who seemed to be speaking on
behalf of citizens' concerns

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:46.367
was Eric Adams.

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:49.901
And I was like, "Wow, this guy
is really compelling."

00:05:49.901 --> 00:05:52.133
And then, as it turned out,
I moved to Brooklyn

00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:53.734
about 20 years ago or so,

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:56.367
and he was the captain
of the police precinct

00:05:56.367 --> 00:05:58.501
in my neighborhood,
so I made a point to go over

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:00.367
and, you know,
just check him out.

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:02.901
And we, you know --
and introduced myself.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:05.300
It's always good to have
a cop friend here and there.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.834
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.834 --> 00:06:08.534
You know, Batman knows that,
you know?

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:12.868
So we went over, and we've been,
you know,

00:06:12.868 --> 00:06:14.000
we've been friends since,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.667
and now he's the mayor
of our great city.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.033
-There you go.
Even a better friend to have.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.200
Look at you. You traded up.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.033
[ Laughter ]
-There you go. There you go.

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:24.601
It worked out well.
But, yeah, I did use him

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:27.167
as a reference for Gordon

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:30.167
because I've never played Gordon
before,

00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:32.501
and those who played before
were not me.

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:35.200
So I wanted to find
justification

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.534
for my playing this role.

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:43.767
And, you know, it's -- I largely
focused on the comics,

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:46.200
and, you know, kind of the
evolution of Gordon, you know,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.767
from 1939,
but, you know, Gotham --

00:06:50.767 --> 00:06:53.234
Gotham's out the window
and there have been three black

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:54.934
police commissioners
of the NYPD,

00:06:54.934 --> 00:06:59.400
and currently we have a black
woman, Commissioner Sewell.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.000
So, you know, there were
touchstones for me

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.033
in justifying my place
in this role.

00:07:08.033 --> 00:07:10.601
And Eric was certainly
one of those, yeah.

00:07:10.601 --> 00:07:12.934
-That's wonderful.
It's wonderful to have a role of

00:07:12.934 --> 00:07:14.901
such importance
in your very safe hands.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.834
I do want to say before you go,

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.300
I think about you
all the time now.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.067
-Oh.
-And I think about you one --

00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:22.133
[ Laughter ]

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:23.367
One, I think about you
all the time.

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:25.067
-I think about you, Seth,
all the time as well.

00:07:25.067 --> 00:07:27.701
-But I'm a huge fan of yours,
but also the reason I think

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:30.968
about you all the time is
we have a writer on this show

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:34.033
who also happens to be named
Jeffrey Wright.

00:07:34.033 --> 00:07:37.601
And in fact, I'm surprised that
he has not come out here yet.

00:07:37.601 --> 00:07:42.701
-But alas, I am here, and there
can only be one Jeffrey Wright.

00:07:42.701 --> 00:07:45.400
So we must battle to the death.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.934
[ Laughter ]

00:07:46.934 --> 00:07:52.934
-Uh, young man, you have just
committed a most grave

00:07:52.934 --> 00:07:55.033
and consequential error,
my friend.

00:07:55.033 --> 00:07:56.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:56.667 --> 00:07:57.901
-You know what?

00:07:57.901 --> 00:08:00.567
You right. I mean, I go by Jeff.

00:08:00.567 --> 00:08:02.567
Jeffrey and Jeff.
-Uh-huh.

00:08:02.567 --> 00:08:05.467
-Two different names,
and you know what they say.

00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:07.601
Two Wrights make a left.

00:08:07.601 --> 00:08:10.133
[ Laughter ]
Don't -- Don't think about it.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:11.701
Hey, Seth, can I go?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:11.701 --> 00:08:13.667
No, I think you better.
Give it up.

00:08:13.667 --> 00:08:15.767
That's Jeff Wright now.
-Jeff Wright.

00:08:15.767 --> 00:08:19.567
-Jeff Wright. And this is
the one, true Jeffrey Wright!

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
"The Batman" premieres in

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:23.467
theaters and IMAX
this Friday, March 4th.

